WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:05.100
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by Skillshare, home to over 20,000

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.200
classes that could teach you a new life skill.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:12.539
Staring at the sky in awe is one of the few
quintessential human experiences.

00:00:12.539 --> 00:00:17.820
Gazing into the vast emptiness of space wondering
where our place in the universe stands.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:22.290
For millennia the mystery of these celestial
bodies captivated the human mind, seeding

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:24.590
religions and mythical stories.

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:29.699
We couldn’t ignore the moon above us, influencing
our tides, or the sun, our benevolent provider,

00:00:29.699 --> 00:00:30.980
burning in the blue sky.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:36.410
At night, individual stars melded together
into a boundless blanket of glittering light,

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:41.100
until we began to notice patterns in their
chaos, guiding us as we explored our world.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:46.020
These star maps helped us make a monumental
discovery, some stars in the sky moved differently

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:47.059
to others.

00:00:47.059 --> 00:00:51.610
Wandering through the night sky, planets like
ours orbiting our sun.

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:58.450
Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter and Saturn,
all visible to the naked eye were discovered

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:02.750
this way by ancient babylonian astronomers
over 4 thousand years ago.

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:07.200
Later Galileo improved the telescope, allowing
him to observe even fainter points in the

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.580
sky, invisible to the naked eye.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:11.189
He discovered Jupiter's moons.

00:01:11.189 --> 00:01:17.180
Ganymede, Callisto, Io and Europa and Saturn’s
moons soon followed mostly discovered by Cassini.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:21.479
Uranus &amp; Neptune had been observed numerous
times through telescopes, but astronomers

00:01:21.479 --> 00:01:24.600
continually mistook them them for stationary
stars or comets.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:29.420
It was only when astronomers noticed the circular
orbit of Uranus that it was accepted as a

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:30.420
planet.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:34.249
Then as it’s orbit was further examined
astronomers noticed deviations, that could

00:01:34.249 --> 00:01:38.959
only be caused by a nearby planet, and thus
Neptune, our solars systems final planet,

00:01:38.959 --> 00:01:40.149
was discovered *on screen joke - Sorry Pluto*

00:01:40.149 --> 00:01:44.310
It tooks thousands of years to just discover
the planets in our solar system, but in the

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:51.009
last 30 years we have discovered over 3717
planets orbiting stars so far away that we

00:01:51.009 --> 00:01:54.200
will never be able to visit them with current
technology.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:58.749
Perhaps the most fascinating planetary system
we have discovered is that of Trappist-1,

00:01:58.749 --> 00:02:03.969
an ultra-cool dwarf star located 40 light
years away from our Sun, which has not only

00:02:03.969 --> 00:02:06.979
1 temperate terrestrial planet, but 7.

00:02:06.979 --> 00:02:10.390
Each of the planets are rocky and between
the size of Earth and Mars.

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:15.450
The star is small and cold compared to ours,
but the planets orbit so closely to the star,

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:19.930
closer than mercury orbits the Sun, that they
all have the possibility of containing liquid

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:20.930
water.

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:24.760
This is a lot of information about planets
that we can’t even see directly.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:29.320
So how do present day astronomers find these,
relatively speaking, tiny objects floating

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:30.470
in space?

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:34.760
The first planets discovered orbiting this
star were discovered by a Belgian team using

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:38.480
the Trappist-south telescope located high
the Chilean mountains.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:42.600
The team had their telescope trained on the
dwarf star waiting for a dimming of it’s

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:47.600
light, a tell tale sign of a planet in orbit
passing in front of the star, and thus blocking

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:48.880
some of it’s light.

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:53.030
But how have we discovered so much about this
system, simply by a dimming of light in its

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:54.030
star?

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:57.750
If we calculate the distance to the star,
using the parallax method, we can discover

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:00.710
a lot more about the planets passing in front
of it.

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:04.440
You can see how the parallax method works
right now by holding your thumb in front of

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.190
you with one eye open.

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:08.610
Now close that eye and open the other.

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:12.710
Did you notice it appeared to move relative
to the background, this is called parallax

00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:16.180
and we can use it to measure the distance
to a star,

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:20.370
but instead of closing our eyes and staring
at the sky, we take measurements of the planets

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:24.900
position from opposite sides of our orbit
around the sun.We know the distance between

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:29.500
these two points, and we can measure the change
in the stars position relative to distant

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:34.120
stars behind it, giving us the parallax angle,
thus we have all the information we need to

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:38.000
calculate the distance to the star using some
simply trigonometry.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:42.080
Now that we know the distance to the star,
we can calculate its mass and size by observing

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:45.710
its apparent brightness and temperature observed
from earth.

00:03:45.710 --> 00:03:50.160
We have models for how stars form, with massive
stars being hotter and brighter than smaller

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:54.460
stars, and so by measuring these values we
can get a lot of information about the star.

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:58.180
That’s how we know Trappist-1 is an ultra-cool
dwarf star.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:02.180
Now that we know more about the star, we discover
what we are really interested in.

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:06.640
The planets size, mass, and whether it resides
in the goldilocks zone where humans could

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:07.640
survive.

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:10.819
To do that we need to know how far away the
planet is from the star.

00:04:10.819 --> 00:04:14.080
We do this using
Kepler’s Third Law which looks like this.

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:18.680
It tells us the orbital period for planet,
that’s how long it takes to orbit it’s

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:19.680
star.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.980
Here G is the Newton’s Gravitational Constant,
capital M is the Star’s mass, lowercase

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:27.449
m is the planet’s mass, and a is the orbital
radius.

00:04:27.449 --> 00:04:31.830
We can ignore the planet’s mass for now,
as it’s miniscule compared to the star’s.

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:37.319
If we do this for Earth, it introduces an
error of just 0.001%.

00:04:37.319 --> 00:04:41.520
The period is measured by seeing the time
between transits, and so we have all the variables

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:44.160
we need to calculate the orbital radius.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:48.210
Now that we know how far away the planet is,
figuring out it’s diameter from the transit

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:49.509
dimming is simple.

00:04:49.509 --> 00:04:53.379
The next bit of information gathering requires
a bit of a perspective shift.

00:04:53.379 --> 00:04:57.199
We are used to the idea that planets orbit
a star, but it would be more accurate to say

00:04:57.199 --> 00:05:01.599
that the planet and star orbit the centre
of mass of the star-planet system, which is

00:05:01.599 --> 00:05:03.629
not at the centre of the star.

00:05:03.629 --> 00:05:07.419
This means the planet’s mass is actually
impacting the orbit of the star around this

00:05:07.419 --> 00:05:08.419
point.

00:05:08.419 --> 00:05:11.340
The larger the mass of the planet the larger
the deviation.

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:15.439
We can measure the magnitude of this deviation
by detecting the doppler shift in the stars

00:05:15.439 --> 00:05:16.940
light frequency.

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:20.319
As the star moves towards us the light becomes
slightly more blue, and when the star moves

00:05:20.319 --> 00:05:22.800
away from us it becomes slightly more red.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:27.669
Using the law of conservation of momentum
we can easily calculate the mass of the planet.

00:05:27.669 --> 00:05:32.099
We know the velocity of the planet from orbital
radius and period measurements, the mass of

00:05:32.099 --> 00:05:36.129
the Star from our distance and luminosity
measurements, and the velocity of the sun

00:05:36.129 --> 00:05:39.180
is measured from these doppler shifts in the
light frequency.

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:43.160
Leaving us with all the information we need
to calculate the mass of the planet.

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:47.909
Using these tools Astronomers have inferred
quite a lot of information about these planets.

00:05:47.909 --> 00:05:52.840
The 7 planets orbit so closely to their star
that a year ranges from just 1.5 days for

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:56.050
the innermost planet to 20 days for the outermost
planet.

00:05:56.050 --> 00:06:00.729
They are so close packed that the planets
will at times appear in each others sky, larger

00:06:00.729 --> 00:06:02.490
than our moon appears in ours.

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:06.789
Maybe the coolest part of the entire thing,
is the planetary system is named after the

00:06:06.789 --> 00:06:09.499
beer the scientist used to toast their discovery.

00:06:09.499 --> 00:06:13.830
But it isn’t all good news, because they
orbit so close, many of the planets will be

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:18.939
tidally locked, like our moon is to the earth,
meaning one side of the planet will constantly

00:06:18.939 --> 00:06:20.330
face the sun.

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:24.309
Which may restrict life to the terminator,
the narrow band of perpetual twilight.

00:06:24.309 --> 00:06:29.330
On top of this, orbiting this closely to a
dwarf star exposes it to radiation from solar

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:33.439
flares, which can strip it’s atmosphere
away, especially if it is not protected by

00:06:33.439 --> 00:06:35.969
a strong magnetic field like earth.

00:06:35.969 --> 00:06:40.330
For now, we don’t have a lot of information
on these planets, beyond their position, period

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:41.330
and mass.

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:45.210
But we’ll know more soon, as the James Webb
Telescope will be able to give us more information

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:49.819
about the atmosphere on these planets by observing
the light that shines through their atmosphere

00:06:49.819 --> 00:06:51.319
during transit.

00:06:51.319 --> 00:06:54.900
By examining the spectrum of light coming
through the atmosphere we can discover what

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:56.800
gases are in its atmosphere.

00:06:56.800 --> 00:06:59.849
Here we have the spectroscopy analysis of
Venus, Earth and Mars.

00:06:59.849 --> 00:07:03.569
All have the fingerprints of carbon dioxide
in their atmosphere, while only Earth has

00:07:03.569 --> 00:07:06.379
the traits of an atmosphere rich in ozone
and water.

00:07:06.379 --> 00:07:11.110
If we see results like these coming from any
of the Trappist-1 planets, a world wide toast

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:13.589
with Belgian beers may be in order.

00:07:13.589 --> 00:07:17.349
Learning how astronomers discover our universe
is fascinating, but it’s unlikely you will

00:07:17.349 --> 00:07:21.610
